{"page_content": "For the biological STP, a concentration in the STP (PECstp) is estimated.\n\nFor fresh and marine water, the PEClocalwater is the sum of the local concentration during the release episode from an STP or during the release period from agriculture use, the regional PEC (see section R.16-1.4.2) and, where relevant, a natural background concentration (relevant for naturally occurring substances).\n\nFor fresh and marine sediments, the PEC is usually estimated from the PEC in water assuming a thermodynamic partitioning equilibrium with water; in case measured data for PECregionalsediment are available these can be used to overwrite the regional contribution to PEClocalsediment.\n\nFor air, the PEClocalair is the sum of the local concentration (at 100m from the point source??) averaged over the year and the regional PEC.\n\nFor soil, the PEClocalsoi is the sum of the local concentration in agricultural soil averaged over 30 days*\u00ae, the regional PEC and, where relevant, the natural background concentration (relevant for naturally occurring substances). Arable land and grassland are together referred\n\n38 Arable land and grassland are together referred to as \u2018agricultural soil\u2019. For exposure of the terrestrial ecosystem PECsoil is calculated for the arable land; PECsoil calculated for the grassland is used for human exposure via the environment.\n\n39 For widespread uses, the point source is the biological STP.\n\n40\n\nIn some cases the initial local concentration (calculated after the last application, not averaged) may need to be used instead of the concentration averaged over 30 days, see section R.16.4.3.5)\n\nto as \u2018agricultural soil\u2019. For exposure of the terrestrial ecosystem PEClocalsoi is calculated for the arable land. Concentrations in other soil compartments and groundwater or at other scales are also calculated as they are needed for the estimation of exposure for secondary poisoning or humans via the environment. They are not used as such in the risk characterisation.\n\nFor secondary poisoning, the concentrations in the food of predators are estimated from the concentrations in the environment and the bioaccumulation. Two concentrations are estimated; one on the basis of the PEC local (in water or soil) and one on the basis of the PEC regional. The model (Supported by EUSES) agreed considers that the PEC for secondary poisoning is the mean of those two concentrations.\n\nFor humans via the environment, a local and a regional exposure are estimated independently. The local scenario is a worst-case scenario.\n\nDetails about calculations are given in Appendix A.16-2.3. The calculations of regional steady- state concentrations (PECregional) are presented in Appendix R.16-3.\n\nIn defining the standard environments, a number of assumptions have to be made with respect to scale and time. These are summarised briefly in the following subsections. More details are given in the relevant sections in Appendix R.16-2.", "metadata": {"doc_id": "6c1d7ad3-dfd4-44e9-a9c4-96b366411218", "content_type": "CompositeElement", "filename": "IR_CSR_R16_V4_FINAL.pdf"}}